## What skoro gives you

- **A model API that reads like ordinary code.** `let y = f(x);` over typed handles
  `Q<B, M>`, where `M` names the manifold or discrete set a quantity lives on. The type system
  stops you from adding a rotation to a vector.
- **Its own differentiation engine.** Every operation supplies a forward value, a vector–Jacobian
  product, and a Hessian–vector product, so first- and second-order methods work over the same
  model. Curved quantities are differentiated in their tangent spaces and updated by
  retraction.
- **Self-describing likelihood terms.** Each density term records *bindings*: labelled edges to
  the nodes that serve as its residual, location, whitening, and Fisher-information root. A
  least-squares solver, a Kalman-style filter, and a Fisher-scoring loop each read the view they
  need from the same model.
- **Two densities over one graph.** The model scores with $p$ and generates with $q$. A term can
  belong to one, the other, or both, which makes importance weights a built-in quantity.
- **burn backends.** Execution runs on [burn](https://burn.dev) tensors: CPU through `flex`,
  GPU through `cubecl`. burn's own autodiff is deliberately not used
  ([why](../../design/bespoke-autodiff/)).

## Who it is for

Four worked examples, each written as it will read when finished, show the kinds of problems skoro
is built for:

- **[Pose estimation](../../guide/example-pose-estimation/)**: MAP over a robot trajectory on
  SE(3) and a landmark map, as nonlinear least squares.
- **[Calibrating a climate model](../../guide/example-climate-mcmc/)**: MCMC over positive physical
  parameters and a covariance factor, with the likelihood running through an ODE.
- **[A sliding-window smoother](../../guide/example-sliding-window/)**: the pose problem as a
  stream, where old poses are marginalized into factors and pruned from the graph.
- **[The yield curve as an affine model](../../guide/example-term-structure/)**: a no-arbitrage
  term-structure model with a differentiable Riccati ODE, fitted to Treasury curves and run daily as
  a Kalman filter built from graph marginalization.

